## 6. PBPE Value Engine – Technical Overview

### 6.1 Input Data Model

The Value Engine accepts JSON-based input structures representing:

- Indicators (name, type, unit, source).
- Entities (countries, regions, sectors).
- Time series data.

### 6.2 Normalization and Weighting

Indicators are normalized to a common scale and weighted according to PBPE
policy configurations. The engine supports:

- Configurable weighting schemes.
- Scenario analysis.
- Sensitivity analysis.

### 6.3 Composite Score Calculation

Composite PBPE scores are calculated by aggregating normalized indicators
within each dimension and then combining dimensions into a single score.
The methodology is transparent and configurable.

---

## 7. PBPE Currency Model – Conceptual Design

### 7.1 PBPE-Linked Currency Units

The Currency Model introduces PBPE-linked units that reflect planetary
performance. These units can be:

- Used for specific PBPE instruments.
- Indexed to PBPE scores.
- Convertible to existing currencies under defined rules.

### 7.2 Adjustment Mechanisms

Currency values can be adjusted based on PBPE performance, creating
incentives for positive ecological and social outcomes.

---

## 8. PBPE Sovereign Bond – Detailed Design

### 8.1 Bond Structure

PBPE Sovereign Bonds are structured with:

- Base coupon rates.
- PBPE performance adjustments.
- Optional PBPE-linked principal bonuses.

### 8.2 Integration with Value Engine

The Sovereign Bond Engine reads PBPE scores from the Value Engine and
recalculates coupon and risk metrics. This creates a direct link between
policy actions and financing conditions.

### 8.3 Risk and Rating Models

Risk models combine traditional metrics (debt-to-GDP, inflation) with PBPE
specific metrics (ecological risk, social stability). Rating agencies can
adopt PBPE-enhanced frameworks.

