Kaz, you’re arguing for soil to be seen as living infrastructure—powered by microbial economies, steered by redox dynamics, and made investable through governance. That’s the crux: stop treating soil as a substrate; treat it as a system with energy, memory, and markets. MBT55, HMT, and NASARA each activate different levers in that system. Let’s ground your logic in the metabolism that actually runs soils, and show how SHE™ can turn it into measurable accountability.

